
Subproblem 1 (Data Loading and Exploration):
  Success Criteria: Successfully load datasets and display summary statistics.
  Constraints: Must handle large datasets efficiently.
  Candidate Approaches:
    A) Load data using pandas: straightforward and efficient for CSV files.
    B) Use Dask for larger-than-memory datasets: adds complexity but handles larger data.
  Selected Approach: A) Load data using pandas, as it is sufficient for the expected dataset size.
  Risk Mitigation: Ensure data is loaded correctly by checking for null values and data types.

Subproblem 2 (Data Preprocessing):
  Success Criteria: Data is cleaned, missing values handled, and features are encoded.
  Constraints: Must maintain data integrity and ensure no information is lost.
  Candidate Approaches:
    A) Impute missing values with mean/median/mode.
    B) Drop rows/columns with excessive missing values.
    C) One-hot encoding for categorical variables.
  Selected Approach: A) Impute missing values and C) One-hot encoding, as they preserve data while preparing it for modeling.
  Risk Mitigation: Validate imputation methods and check for data leakage.

Subproblem 3 (Exploratory Data Analysis):
  Success Criteria: Generate insights about data distributions and relationships.
  Constraints: Must summarize findings in a text-based format.
  Candidate Approaches:
    A) Descriptive statistics (mean, median, mode).
    B) Correlation matrix to identify relationships.
  Selected Approach: A) Descriptive statistics and B) Correlation matrix, as they provide a comprehensive overview of the data.
  Risk Mitigation: Ensure interpretations are accurate and based on statistical significance.

Subproblem 4 (Model Selection and Training):
  Success Criteria: Train a model that minimizes log loss on validation data.
  Constraints: Must avoid overfitting and ensure generalization.
  Candidate Approaches:
    A) Logistic Regression: simple and interpretable.
    B) Random Forest: handles non-linear relationships well.
    C) Gradient Boosting: powerful but may require tuning.
  Selected Approach: B) Random Forest, as it balances complexity and performance without extensive tuning.
  Risk Mitigation: Use cross-validation to ensure model robustness.
