## Methodological Decision Framework:

### Decision 1: Preprocessing & Feature Engineering
Candidate Methods:
  A) Imputation: Mean/Median/Mode imputation for missing values.
  B) Removal: Drop rows/columns with excessive missing values.
  C) Encoding: One-hot encoding for categorical variables.
  D) Scaling: Standardization or Min-Max scaling for numeric features.

Suitability Analysis:
  - Data characteristics: Missing values, categorical vs. numeric features.
  - Assumption satisfaction: Imputation assumes data is missing at random.
  - Failure modes: Imputation may introduce bias if not handled correctly.

Selected Method: Imputation for missing values and one-hot encoding for categorical variables, justified by the presence of missing data and the need for categorical representation.

Quality validation: Check for remaining missing values and ensure all categorical variables are encoded.

### Decision 2: Model Selection
Candidate Methods:
  A) Random Forest: Handles non-linear relationships and interactions.
  B) Gradient Boosting: Effective for imbalanced datasets.
  C) Neural Networks: Suitable for complex patterns but requires more data.
  D) Logistic Regression: Simple and interpretable but may underfit.

Suitability Analysis:
  - Data characteristics: Class imbalance, feature types.
  - Assumption satisfaction: Random Forest and Gradient Boosting do not assume normality.
  - Failure modes: Neural networks may overfit with limited data.

Selected Method: Random Forest due to its robustness to overfitting and ability to handle class imbalance.

Quality validation: Use cross-validation to assess model performance.

### Decision 3: Validation Strategy
Candidate Methods:
  A) K-Fold Cross-Validation: Provides a robust estimate of model performance.
  B) Stratified K-Fold: Ensures class distribution is maintained across folds.
  C) Leave-One-Out: Useful for small datasets but computationally expensive.

Suitability Analysis:
  - Data characteristics: Size of the dataset and class distribution.
  - Assumption satisfaction: Stratified K-Fold maintains class balance.
  - Failure modes: K-Fold may not capture rare classes effectively.

Selected Method: Stratified K-Fold Cross-Validation to ensure class balance.

Quality validation: Monitor performance metrics across folds.
