## Methodological Decision Framework:

### Decision 1: Preprocessing & Feature Engineering
**Candidate Methods:**
- A) **Mean/Median Imputation**: Simple and fast; assumes missing values are random.
- B) **KNN Imputation**: More sophisticated; uses nearest neighbors but computationally expensive.
- C) **Predictive Modeling for Imputation**: Uses regression models to predict missing values; complex but can be more accurate.

**Suitability Analysis:**
- Data characteristics: Missing values are present in several features.
- Assumption satisfaction: Mean/Median imputation is suitable for MCAR; KNN and predictive modeling require more analysis.
- Failure modes: KNN may fail with high dimensionality; predictive modeling may overfit.

**Selected Method:** Mean/Median Imputation for initial handling of missing values, with KNN as a secondary option if needed.

### Decision 2: Model Selection
**Candidate Methods:**
- A) **Linear Regression**: Simple, interpretable; assumes linearity.
- B) **Random Forest**: Handles non-linearity and interactions; robust to outliers.
- C) **Gradient Boosting (XGBoost)**: Powerful for structured data; requires tuning.

**Suitability Analysis:**
- Data characteristics: Mixed types (numeric and categorical), potential non-linearity.
- Assumption satisfaction: Linear regression assumptions may not hold; Random Forest and XGBoost are more flexible.
