#### Handling Missing Values:
The previous attempt to fill missing values resulted in a `ChainedAssignmentError`. This error occurs when trying to modify a DataFrame in place while working with a view of the DataFrame rather than a copy. To resolve this, we will avoid using the `inplace=True` parameter and instead directly assign the filled values back to the DataFrame.

### Revised Implementation of Missing Value Handling:
We will modify the code to handle missing values without using `inplace=True`. Here’s the corrected approach:

```python
# Handling missing values in the training dataset
# For features with high missing values, we will consider dropping them or using a specific imputation strategy.

# Drop features with excessive missing values (> 20% missing)
threshold = 0.2
missing_train_percentage = train_data.isnull().mean()
features_to_drop = missing_train_percentage[missing_train_percentage > threshold].index.tolist()
train_data_cleaned = train_data.drop(columns=features_to_drop)

# Impute missing values for remaining features
# For numerical features, we can use mean/median imputation
numerical_features = train_data_cleaned.select_dtypes(include=['float64', 'int64']).columns
for feature in numerical_features:
    train_data_cleaned[feature] = train_data_cleaned[feature].fillna(train_data_cleaned[feature].median())

# For categorical features, we can use the mode
categorical_features = train_data_cleaned.select_dtypes(include=['object']).columns
for feature in categorical_features:
    train_data_cleaned[feature] = train_data_cleaned[feature].fillna(train_data_cleaned[feature].mode()[0])

# Check missing values after imputation
print("Missing Values After Imputation in Training Data:\n", train_data_cleaned.isnull().sum())
```

