## Methodological Decision Framework:

### Decision 1: Preprocessing & Feature Engineering
Candidate Methods:
  A) **Imputation**: Fill missing values using mean/median/mode or advanced techniques like KNN.
     - Assumptions: Missingness is not informative.
     - Pros: Simple to implement.
     - Cons: May introduce bias if not handled correctly.
     - Computational Cost: Low.
  
  B) **Encoding**: One-hot encoding for categorical features or target encoding.
     - Assumptions: Categorical features are independent.
     - Pros: Captures non-linear relationships.
     - Cons: High dimensionality with one-hot encoding.
     - Computational Cost: Moderate.
  
  C) **Scaling**: Standardization or Min-Max scaling for numeric features.
     - Assumptions: Features are normally distributed.
     - Pros: Improves convergence in models sensitive to feature scales.
     - Cons: Sensitive to outliers.
     - Computational Cost: Low.

Suitability Analysis:
  - Data characteristics: Missing values, categorical features, and numeric features.
  - Assumption satisfaction: Check if assumptions hold for the selected methods.
  - Failure modes: Imputation may fail if missingness is informative.
